---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI credentials" activeNav="/cli/credentials">
  <h1>Credentials</h1>
  <p>
    A credential is signing or publishing material: a keystore, a distribution certificate, a
    provisioning profile, a service account key. Each one carries an encrypted JSON payload
    and, for the types that need it, an attached file. Credentials belong to the team and are
    attached to build profiles on an identifier.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl credentials list     [--app APP]
appmctl credentials get      [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID
appmctl credentials create   [--app APP] --type TYPE --profile PROFILE [--data-file FILE|-] [--file FILE]
appmctl credentials update   [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID --data-file FILE|-
appmctl credentials delete   [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID
appmctl credentials download [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID [-o FILE]
appmctl credentials upload   [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID --file FILE
appmctl credentials verify   [--app APP] CREDENTIAL_ID --status verified|failed [options]
appmctl credentials types`} />

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl credentials list" capture="credentials-list" />
  <p>
    <code>expires_at</code> is blank for material that does not expire, such as an Android
    keystore. For certificates it is the value to watch in CI.
  </p>

  <h3>Available types</h3>
  <p>
    Types are fixed by the platform. <code>create</code> rejects a slug that is not on this
    list, so check it before scripting a new credential:
  </p>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl credentials types" capture="credentials-types" />

  <h2>Secret data never goes in an argument</h2>
  <p>
    Credential payloads are passed with <code>--data-file</code>, from a file or from stdin
    with <code>-</code>. An inline <code>--data JSON</code> flag still exists but is
    deprecated: arguments are visible to other users through the process list and are
    routinely written into CI logs.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# From stdin, so the payload never becomes an argument
printf '%s' '{"store_password":"...","key_alias":"customer","key_password":"..."}' | \\
  appmctl credentials create \\
    --type android_keystore --profile production \\
    --data-file - --file ./release.jks

# From a file that a secret manager wrote
appmctl credentials create --type ios_certificate --profile appstore \\
  --data-file ./cert-payload.json --file ./distribution.p12`} />
  <p>
    Dry-run confirms the body is withheld. The CLI reports its size and nothing else:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --dry-run credentials create --type android_keystore \\
  --profile production --data-file ./payload.json

# dry-run: no request sent
# method: POST
# url: https://manager.example.com/api/v1/acme-mobile/apps/customer-app/credentials
# body: credential JSON (125 bytes; redacted)`} />
  <p>
    Inline bodies are written to a mode-<code>0600</code> file in a mode-<code>0700</code>
    temporary directory before <code>curl</code> reads them, so the payload is never an
    argument to <code>curl</code> either.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotating a payload</h2>
  <p>
    <code>update</code> replaces the encrypted payload and requires
    <code>--data-file</code>. The attached file is replaced separately with
    <code>upload</code>, so rotating a password and rotating a keystore are two distinct
    operations:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# New passwords, same keystore file
appmctl credentials update 019fc789-47aa-703d-9eaa-f9ca4fea85a2 --data-file ./new-payload.json

# New keystore file, same payload
appmctl credentials upload 019fc789-47aa-703d-9eaa-f9ca4fea85a2 --file ./release-2027.jks`} />

  <h2>Downloading material</h2>
  <p>
    <code>download</code> writes the attached file. It needs the
    <code>credentials:reveal</code> scope on top of read access, and the
    <code>manage_credentials</code> team permission — reading a keystore is a different act
    from listing that one exists:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl credentials download 019fc789-47aa-703d-9eaa-f9ca4fea85a2 -o release.jks`} />
  <p>
    On a shared runner, delete the file when the build step is done. The CLI cleans up its own
    temporary state but has no say over a path you chose.
  </p>

  <h2>Verifying an external vault reference</h2>
  <p>
    A credential can reference material in an external secret manager instead of storing it here.
    App Manager holds only the reference, and <strong>it never reaches into your vault</strong> — it
    has no network path to it and no business holding the credentials that would open it. So the
    question "does this reference still resolve?" can only be answered by something that can reach
    the vault: your build.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>verify</code> is how that answer gets recorded. Your pipeline tries to resolve the secret,
    then reports the verdict it reached. The CLI transmits it; it does not judge:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl credentials verify CREDENTIAL_ID --status verified|failed \\
  [--error TEXT] [--expires-at DATE] [--metadata JSON]`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--status verified|failed</code></td>
        <td>The verdict. Required — there is no default, because a default would be the CLI guessing.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--error TEXT</code></td>
        <td>Why resolution failed. Required with <code>failed</code>, rejected with <code>verified</code>. Up to 1000 characters.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--expires-at DATE</code></td>
        <td>An expiry your build read out of the vault, written onto the credential. Only with <code>verified</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--metadata JSON</code></td>
        <td>A flat JSON object of string values describing what was resolved. Replaces the stored metadata wholesale.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>In a pipeline</h3>
  <p>
    The shape that matters: resolve, then report either way. A build that only reports success leaves
    a broken reference looking fine until a release needs it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`set -uo pipefail

CRED=019fc789-47aa-703d-9eaa-f9ca4fea85a2

if secret=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \\
      --secret-id "$VAULT_ARN" --query SecretString --output text 2>err.txt); then
  printf '%s' "$secret" > ./distribution.p12

  appmctl credentials verify "$CRED" --status verified \\
    --expires-at "$(openssl pkcs12 -in ./distribution.p12 -nokeys -passin pass: 2>/dev/null |
                    openssl x509 -noout -enddate | sed 's/notAfter=//')" \\
    --metadata '{"region":"eu-central-1","resolved_by":"github-actions"}'
else
  appmctl credentials verify "$CRED" --status failed \\
    --error "$(head -c 900 err.txt)"
  exit 1
fi`} />
  <p>
    Contradictions are caught before the request, not as a <code>422</code> afterwards:
    <code>failed</code> without <code>--error</code>, <code>--error</code> on a
    <code>verified</code> verdict, <code>--expires-at</code> on a failure, and a
    <code>--status</code> outside the two reportable values. <code>unverified</code> is a stored
    state, not something you can report.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only a credential stored in your own vault can be verified — reporting on a locally stored one
    answers <code>422</code>, since there is no external reference to resolve.
  </p>

  <h3>What the platform does with the verdict</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>verified</code> stamps the verification time. The badge in the
      <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">vault</a> clears.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>failed</code> stores the reason and shows <strong>Verification failed</strong> in the
      validity column.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The first</strong> transition into <code>failed</code> raises the
      <code>credential.verification_failed</code> event for
      <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhooks</a> and
      <a href="/platform/integrations/notifications">chat notifications</a>. A build retrying in a
      loop does not re-notify — only the transition is newsworthy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--metadata</code> replaces the stored object, bumps its version, and marks its source as
      <code>pipeline</code> rather than <code>user</code>, so a value your build discovered is
      distinguishable from one someone typed into the form.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Verification is never automatic. Nothing expires a verdict, so a reference verified six months
    ago still reads as verified — run it as part of a release preflight rather than trusting an old
    badge.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl credentials delete 019fc789-47aa-703d-9eaa-f9ca4fea85a2`} />
  <p>
    A credential attached to a build profile cannot be deleted while builds depend on it;
    detach it first through the web UI or by updating the profile.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Scope</th><th>Team permission</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>types</code></td><td><code>credentials:read</code></td><td><code>view</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>upload</code></td><td><code>credentials:write</code></td><td><code>manage_credentials</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>download</code></td><td><code>credentials:reveal</code></td><td><code>manage_credentials</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>verify</code></td><td><code>credentials:verify</code></td><td><code>manage_credentials</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Keys used by build pipelines usually need <code>credentials:reveal</code>, since a signing
    step has to read the material. Keys used for reporting build status should not have it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>credentials:verify</code> is deliberately separate from <code>reveal</code>: reporting
    whether a reference resolves is not reading the secret behind it. A pipeline that resolves its
    own secrets from its own vault needs <code>verify</code> and never needs
    <code>reveal</code> — App Manager is not holding anything for it to read.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
